# Naming and Single-Responsibility Audit: Naming Mismatch and Misleading Names
File: openOODA/audits/naming/mcode/misnaming_findings.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/naming-audit-mcode.md` (788 lines). Part 2 of 7.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

## 2. Defect class (a) — Naming mismatch / misleading names

### 2.1 `openOODA-core/std` — a "standard library" that carries the kernel (F5)

`openOODA-core/std/anchor.oo:19` declares the `hw/` domain to hold `bare_metal, drivers_hal, unikernel`.
`openOODA-core/std/hw/anchor.oo:3` simultaneously declares the *same* directory to be "a theoretical-foundation library … No
public API code -- the directory is documentation and verification rules." Both cannot hold: `std/hw/` contains **112 `.oo`
files with real implementations** across 9 subdirectories.

Direct duplication of named sibling repos in `openOODA-kernel`:

Table columns: `std` file (Academy logline) | `openOODA-kernel` counterpart
- `std/hw/unikernel/firecracker.oo:3` — "Manages Firecracker MicroVMs via REST API socket, vsock streams, and sub-millisecond
  snapshot restores." | `ookernel-unikernel/README.md:3` — "Single-address-space Firecracker and QEMU microVM target"
- `std/hw/unikernel/virtio.oo:3` — "Implements VirtIO MMIO transport, Split Virtqueue descriptors, and VirtIO-Net / VirtIO-Blk /
  VirtIO-Console drivers." | `oodrv-virtio/README.md:3` — "Ring-3 VirtIO block, net, gpu, and input split/packed virtqueue
  driver suite"
- `std/hw/drivers_hal/lapic_driver.oo:1` — "x86_64 Local APIC and IO-APIC Hardware Driver" | `ookernel-hal-x86/README.md:3` —
  "x86_64 GDT/IDT, SYSCALL/SYSRET, LAPIC/IOAPIC, and SMP startup HAL"
- `std/hw/drivers_hal/gicv3_driver.oo:1` — "ARM GICv3 (Generic Interrupt Controller v3) Driver" |
  `ookernel-hal-arm64/README.md:3` — "ARM64 EL1 exception vectors, GICv3 interrupt controller, and PSCI SMP HAL"
- `std/hw/drivers_hal/uefi_framebuffer.oo:1` — "UEFI 64-Bit Linear Framebuffer Graphics Driver" |
  `oodrv-display-gop/README.md:3` — "Ring-3 UEFI GOP, VirtIO-GPU, and DRM scanout driver"
- `std/hw/bare_metal/cnode_tree.oo` — "Capability Derivation Tree (CDT) … cascading subtree revocation" |
  `ookernel-ob/ob/cnode_tree.oo` — **same logline verbatim**
- `std/hw/bare_metal/cspace_table.oo` — "CSpace root table directory storing and retrieving full 256-bit capability descriptors"
  | `ookernel-ob/ob/cspace_table.oo` — **same logline verbatim**

`std/ipc/wire.oo:3` implements an IPC wire codec — the same duty as `ookernel-ipc/README.md:3` — yet `std/ipc/` has **no
`anchor.oo`** and is not in the declared 8-domain public surface (`std/anchor.oo:36-46`).

**This is the closest structural analogue to the `oomedia` defect class anywhere in the fleet**: a repo whose name states one
domain, carrying a second, separately-named domain.

**Verdict: SPLIT** → `std` (the 8 declared domains) + `std-hw` (the 112 driver/unikernel files, Layer 1).

### 2.2 `openOODA-OS/ooimage` — claims three responsibilities, implements one (F6)

`openOODA-OS/ooimage/README.md:76` claims:

> "Deterministic OS & 3-tier OCI container image builder … + Level 1 Linux+OODA ISO and Level 2 `ookernel` UEFI/Firecracker
> images."

Table columns: # | Claimed responsibility | Declared at | Implemented?
- 1 | 3-tier OCI container image builder | `README.md:14,76` | **Yes** — `ooimage/containers/ooda_core.oo:18-33` returns a
  Containerfile string; `ooimage/containers/ooda_inference.oo:34-42` returns a Quadlet unit string
- 2 | Level 1 Linux+OODA ISO builder | `README.md:76` | **No** — 0 source hits for
  `iso | uefi | firecracker | \.img | squashfs | bootloader | grub`
- 3 | Level 2 `ookernel` UEFI/Firecracker image builder | `README.md:76` | **No** — same 0 hits

Two further overclaims in the same file:
- **"Quadlet *manager*"`** — there is no lifecycle management. The only Quadlet interaction is a string lookup (`
  ooimage/containers/anchor.oo:18`). No install/start/stop/enable path exists.
- **"Image layer table"** — the table is the generic `core/{types,store,machine}.oo` boilerplate shared with every OS repo,
  differentiated only by an IPC magic number.

**Bonus defect in the same repo (port mismatch):** `ooimage/containers/ooda_inference.oo:24` sets `ENV OODA_AID_PORT="11434"`
and `:28` declares `EXPOSE 11434`, but `:39` emits `Environment=OODA_AID_PORT=11435` in the Quadlet. `ooaid/net/config.oo:54`
uses `11434`. The Quadlet would start the container on the wrong port.

**Verdict: SPLIT + RENAME.** Ship `oocontainer` (the generator that actually exists) now; open `ooiso` and `ookernelimg` later
to make the ISO/UEFI claims true. `ooflash` correctly sidesteps the name axis — it names the *action* (flash), not the
*artifact* (image), so it does not collide with `ooimage` (image **builder**).

### 2.3 `openOODA-OS/oorealm` — "realm" spans three unrelated substrates

`openOODA-OS/oorealm/README.md:5` — "Flathub OSTree realms, the 3 canonical rootless OCI server containers (`ooda-core`,
`ooda-buildbox`, `ooda-inference`), and Steam/Proton + WinRealm IOMMU gaming realms." `oorealm/core/engine.oo:8` — "validate
tier-specific capability requirements (Tier 1 Flathub, Tier 2 OCI, Tier 3 Proton/WinRealm IOMMU)".

Three domains (desktop app distribution / server containers / gaming anti-cheat virtualization). `oorealm/cli.oo:66`
additionally hardcodes `"containers":["ooda-core","ooda-buildbox","ooda-inference"]` — **the same canonical tier list
`ooimage/containers/anchor.oo:15-17` owns**. Two repos independently assert the canonical container composition: a
single-source-of-truth defect that will silently drift.

**Verdict: SPLIT** → `ooflathub`, `oocontainerrealm`, `oowinrealm`; shared realm engine becomes a library.

### 2.4 `openOODA-DE/oofiles` — named for a file manager, actually a 6-domain shell host

`openOODA-DE/oofiles/README.md:3` — "Dual-pane spatial and Miller-column file manager". But `oofiles/README.md:64-69` declares
six absorbed domains:

```
| `oofiles`    | — (canonical)  | `list`  |
| `ooview`     | `ooview` repo  | `open`  |
| `ooedit`     | `ooedit` repo  | `open`  |
| `ooclip`     | `ooclip` repo  | `copy`  |
| `ooshot`     | `ooshot` repo  | `capture`|
| `ootaskmgr`  | `ootaskmgr` repo | `ps`  |
```

File manager + document viewer + code editor + clipboard broker + screenshot capture + process inspector. **Verdict: RENAME →
`ooshell`** (see §2.5 — only one DE repo may claim `ooshell`).

### 2.5 `openOODA-DE/oopanel` — named for a panel, actually the DE shell host

`openOODA-DE/oopanel/README.md:3` — "Top status panel, workspace pill strip, system tray, clock, and active capability indicator
bar". It additionally absorbs `ooshelf`, `ootoast`, `oocockpit` (an entire OODA command deck) and `oolaunch` (
`oopanel/README.md:63-67`). A panel that dispatches to a command deck is a shell.

`openOODA-DE/ooportal` vs `oopanel` differ by two characters in the middle and are both DE shell-surface hosts — **high
confusion risk**.

**Verdict:** rename one of `oofiles`/`oopanel` → **`ooshell`**; rename `ooportal` → **`oogrant`** (it is the
capability-grant/privacy surface, per `ooportal/core/persona/dispatch.oo:17`).

### 2.6 `openOODA-DE/oobazaar` — a "bazaar" hosting four install backends

`openOODA-DE/oobazaar/README.md:5-9` browses/installs: native `opm` packages, Tier-1 Flathub/Bazaar Flatpaks, Tier-2
Podman-style rootless containers, Tier-3 Steam/Proton/WinRealm games. Four provisioning backends in one store binary. **Verdict:
SPLIT** per backend, or keep one store with thin backend adapters.

### 2.7 `openOODA-apps/oovm-ui` — declares a KVM VM manager, implements a `/proc` monitor

`openOODA-apps/oovm-ui/README.md:3` — "**Virtual machine manager UI over KVM cells**". The only domain page in the repo is
`oovm-ui/monitor.oo` (42 lines), which reads `/proc/meminfo` and `/proc/loadavg` and returns one string (
`mem_total=… mem_avail=… cached=… load1=… load5=… load15=… tasks=…`). Negative evidence: no `qemu`, `kvm`, `libvirt`, `virt`,
`cell`, `domain`, `vcpu`, or `snapshot` string anywhere in the repo.

"VM" is ambiguous *and* misleading — it is not "vector markup" nor "volume manager"; the name asserts a virtual-machine domain
the code does not implement. The real behaviour is a host resource monitor.

**Verdict: RENAME → `oohostmon`.** (`oomonitor` is rejected — collides with `openOODA-tools/oomonitor`.)

### 2.8 `openOODA-core` — repo name ≠ shipped binary name

Table columns: repo | Makefile `BIN` | CI artifact | Disposition
- `lsp` | — | `dist/ooda-lsp-linux-x86_64` (`lsp/.github/workflows/ci.yml:150`) | **RENAME → `ooda-lsp`**
- `mcp` | `dist/mcp` (`mcp/Makefile:26`) | `dist/ooda-mcp-linux-x86_64` (`mcp/.github/workflows/ci.yml:141`) | **RENAME →
  `ooda-mcp`** (two names for one binary inside one repo)

Consumers carry both names: `cli/README.md:28` says "ooda-mcp / ooda-lsp / opm" while `cli/persona/router_run.oo:21-22` spawns
bare `"lsp"` and `"mcp"`; `cli/build/spawn.oo:35-36` papers over it with an explicit legacy-alias fallback commented "Old
on-disk names only." `openOODA-core/tui/mcp_client.oo:79` carries a **five-step sequential legacy-name fallback** for one
binary.

### 2.9 `openOODA-core/oodar` — three-way metadata contradiction

| Claim | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Library name | `liboodar-core.a` | `oodar/README.md:21` |
| Library name | `liboodar.a` | `oodar/scripts/Makefile:3`, `oodar/anchor.oo:5`, CI `oodar/.github/workflows/ci.yml:55` |
| Layer | `Layer 0` | `oodar/README.md:21` |
| Layer | `Layer 1 Host Substrate` | `oodar/anchor.oo:1` |
| `.c` file count | `111 .c files` | `oodar/anchor.oo:3,5` — actual 130 non-`qa` files, 124 `#include`s in `oodar.c` |

### 2.10 `openOODA-kernel/oodrv-net` — name promises NICs, code delivers a generic ring

`oodrv-net/README.md:3` — "**Ring-3 e1000e, RTL8125, and VirtIO-net packet ring driver**". `e1000e`/`RTL8125` appear **only** in
the persona description string (`persona/self_set.oo:60-62`) and in zero implementation files. The only domain artifact is
`core/drv_net_parse.oo:3` — "7-word wire codec for the ethernet frame header". No descriptor ring, no MMIO, no PHY.

**Verdict: RENAME → `oodrv-io`** (its own union persona is already `oodrv-io`, `persona/self_set.oo:77`).

### 2.11 `openOODA-kernel/ookernel-hal-x86` — an x86-named host for three architectures

`ookernel-hal-x86/persona/anchor.oo:123` — "Canonical unified HAL surface for x86_64, ARM64, and RISC-V". The directory and
binary say x86 only, while `ookernel-hal-arm64` and `ookernel-hal-riscv` are absorbed by it. **Verdict: RENAME → `oohal`.**

### 2.12 `openOODA-agents/oooda-engine` — a stutter, plus an alias stolen by another host

`oooda-engine` reads as `oo` + `ooda-engine`, i.e. a stutter against the project's own `oo` prefix convention. Worse, the alias
`engine` is claimed by two hosts:

- `openOODA-agents/oooda-engine/persona/meta.oo:24` — `if id == "oooda-engine" { return "engine"; }`
- `openOODA-agents/ooherdr/persona/meta.oo:34` — `if name == "engine" { return "ooherdr"; }`

`--persona engine` resolves to a different binary depending on which host is invoked.

**Verdict: RENAME → `ooooda`** and drop the `engine` alias from one of the two hosts.

### 2.13 `openOODA-agents/oocoder` — a vendor name used as a top-level persona id

`openOODA-agents/oocoder/persona/meta.oo:19,36,55` registers persona id `oodeepseek`, alias `deepseek`, described as "DeepSeek
model coding persona: prompts and review backed by deepseek-coder endpoint". A single vendor's model binding inside a generic
code-analysis agent, with a hardcoded port `(:11435)` at `oocoder/ooda.pkg:8`. **Verdict: RENAME persona `oodeepseek` →
`oocoder-deepseek`.**

### 2.14 Redundant org suffixes inside the cloud org

`oodns-cloud`, `oosched-cloud`, `ooregistry-cloud` all restate the org name that already appears in the repo path (
`openOODA-cloud/…`). The majority style in the same org — `oocdn`, `oobilling`, `oogateway`, `oomesh`, `oorelay`, `oostore`,
`ootelemetry` — carries no suffix. The suffix is also load-bearing: `openOODA-tools/oodns` already exists, so `oodns-cloud` is
the only thing preventing a stem collision with L3 tooling.

**Verdict: RENAME** → `oodns`, `oosched`, `ooregistry`.

### 2.15 `openOODA-tools/oote` — collides with the reserved name `ootext`

`zero_symlinks_directive.oot:14` reserves `oosort`/`ootext` as the canonical text-processing host.
`openOODA-tools/oote/README.md:1` is "**openOODA Theming Engine**" — a colour/theme engine, not text processing. The reserved
name `ootext` therefore has no home, while the near-identical `oote` is occupied by an unrelated tool. **Verdict:** create the
canonical `ootext` host, and rename the theme engine to `ootheme` (or `oopalette`) to free the axis.

### 2.16 `openOODA-tools/oomd5` — a repo whose own title says "LEGACY"

`openOODA-tools/oomd5/README.md:1` — "**oomd5: Native MD5 LEGACY**". It is simultaneously a standalone repo, a published catalog
package, and a persona of *both* `oob3sum` and `oohash`. MD5 is a legacy digest with no correctness role. **Verdict: ARCHIVE**
(absorbed into the digest host); the `md5sum`/`md5` **persona tokens** may remain for CLI compatibility per RFC 0012 §2.2.

### 2.17 `openOODA-OS/ooimage` vs `openOODA-apps/oomedia` vs `openOODA-apps/ooflash` — the "image" name axis

Three repos use image language for three different things: `ooimage` **builds** container/ISO images; `oomedia` **played** media
and **wrote** images to devices; `ooflash` **writes** images to devices. A user typing `ooimage` to flash a USB stick would be
wrong by two layers. `ooflash` is the correct verb-name and resolves this; `ooimage` should be narrowed to `oocontainer` (§2.2).

### 2.18 Names that are clean (explicitly checked, no finding)

- `openOODA-apps/oopass` (credential vault, `oopass/audit.oo:3`) vs `openOODA-DE/oodeck` (slide deck, `oodeck/slides.oo:3`) —
  different domains, no collision.
- `openOODA-apps/oodaw` (audio workstation, `oodaw/session.oo:3`) vs `openOODA-DE/ooedit` (code editor) — different domains,
  different orgs.
- `openOODA-cloud/oostore` (object/bucket storage, `oostore/core/bucket.oo:3`) vs `openOODA-cloud/oostore-cas`
  (content-addressed blob store + PQC provenance, `oostore-cas/core/cas.oo:3`) — genuinely distinct domains; the near-collision
  is resolved by archiving `oostore-cas` into `oostore` (§4).
- `openOODA-cloud/oobilling` (commercial metering, `oobilling/core/meter.oo:3`) vs `openOODA-agents/ooquota` (agent token
  burn-rate, `ooquota/core/pace.oo:3`) vs `openOODA-cloud/ootelemetry` (metrics) — three different currencies; no collision.
- `openOODA-cloud/ooconsent` and `openOODA-agents/ooconsensus` are **different names** — no cross-org collision (the hypothesis
  was tested and rejected).
